
### Tokenized State Machine

------

#### **Overview**

The `tokenized_state_machine!` macro models concurrent systems via state machines with tokenization.

- **Sharding Strategies**: Define how state fields are split into tokens for verification.
- **Generated Tokens**: Each field in the `fields` block produces a token type (e.g., `M::f`) based on its strategy.

------

#### **Fields Block Structure**

Declare state fields with `#[sharding(...)]` to specify tokenization:

```
tokenized_state_machine!{    
  X {        
    fields {            
      #[sharding(variable)] // Strategy = variable → generates `X::counter` token            
      pub counter: int,        
    }        
    ...  
  }
}
```

------

#### **Sharding Strategies & Traits**

| Strategy   | Token Trait                 | Collection Helper Struct | Notes                                                        |
| :--------- | :-------------------------- | :----------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `variable` | `UniqueValueToken<V>`       | —                        | The value of the token is the same as the value of the field. Having multiple such tokens at the same time (for the same field) is an impossible state. |
| `constant` | *(No token)*                | —                        | —                                                            |
| `option`   | `UniqueValueToken<V>`       | —                        | When `field` is `None`, this corresponds to no token existing, while when `field` is `Some(v)`, this corresponds to a token of value `v` existing. Having multiple such tokens at the same time is an impossible state. |
| `map`      | `UniqueKeyValueToken<K, V>` | `MapToken<K, V, M::f>`   | Every token represents a *single* key-value pair (given by the `.key()` and `.value()` functions on the `UniqueKeyValueToken`trait). The value of the field on the global state is the map given by the collection of key-value pairs. |
| `set`      | `UniqueElementToken<V>`     | `SetToken<V, M::f>`      | Every token represents a *single* element of the set (given by the `.element()` function on the `UniqueElementToken`. The value of the field on the global state is the set given by the collection of all such elements. |
| `multiset` | `UniqueElementToken<V>`     | `MultisetToken<V, M::f>` | Every token represents a *single* element of the multiset (given by the `.element()` function on the `UniqueElementToken` trait). The value of the field on the global state is the multiset given by the collection of all such elements. |
| `bool`     | `UniqueSimpleToken`         | —                        | When `field` is `false`, this corresponds to no token existing, while when `field` is `true`, this corresponds to a token existing. The token doesn’t contain any additional data. Having multiple such tokens at the same time is an impossible state. |
| `count`    | `CountToken`                | —                        | The global value of the `field` is the sum of the counts of all the tokens. |

------

#### **Token Traits**

##### **Core Traits**

1. **`ElementToken<Element>`**

   ```
   pub trait ElementToken<Element>: Sized {
       // Required methods
       spec fn instance_id(&self) -> InstanceId;
       spec fn element(&self) -> Element;
       proof fn arbitrary() -> tracked s : Self;
   }
   ```

2. **`ValueToken<Value>`**

   ```
   pub trait ValueToken<Value>: Sized {
       // Required methods
       spec fn instance_id(&self) -> InstanceId;
       spec fn value(&self) -> Value;
       proof fn agree(tracked &self, tracked other: &Self)
       	requires
   			self.instance_id() == other.instance_id(),
   		ensures
   			self.value() == other.value(),
       proof fn arbitrary() -> tracked s : Self;
   }
   ```

3. **`KeyValueToken<Key, Value>`**

   ```
   pub trait KeyValueToken<Key, Value>: Sized {
       // Required methods
       spec fn instance_id(&self) -> InstanceId;
       spec fn key(&self) -> Key;
       spec fn value(&self) -> Value;
       proof fn agree(tracked &self, tracked other: &Self)
           requires
               self.instance_id() == other.instance_id(),
               self.key() == other.key(),
           ensures
               self.value() == other.value(),
       proof fn arbitrary() -> tracked s : Self;
   }
   ```

4. **`SimpleToken`**

   ```
   pub trait SimpleToken: Sized {
       // Required methods
       spec fn instance_id(&self) -> InstanceId;
       proof fn arbitrary() -> tracked s : Self;
   }
   ```

5. **`CountToken`**

   ```
   pub trait CountToken: Sized {
       // Required methods
       spec fn instance_id(&self) -> InstanceId;
       spec fn count(&self) -> nat;
       proof fn join(tracked &mut self, tracked other: Self)
       	requires
   			old(self).instance_id() == other.instance_id(),
   		ensures
   			self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
   			self.count() == old(self).count() + other.count(),
       proof fn split(tracked &mut self, count: nat) -> tracked token : Self
       	requires
               count <= old(self).count(),
           ensures
               self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
               self.count() == old(self).count() - count,
               token.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
               token.count() == count,
       proof fn weaken_shared(tracked &self, count: nat) -> tracked s : &Self
       	requires
               count <= self.count(),
           ensures
               s.instance_id() == self.instance_id(),
               s.count() == count,
       proof fn arbitrary() -> tracked s : Self;
   }
   ```

##### **Uniqueness Traits**

1. **`UniqueValueToken<V>`**

   ```
   pub trait UniqueValueToken<Value>: ValueToken<Value> {
       // Required method
       proof fn unique(tracked &mut self, tracked other: &Self)
       	ensures
               self.instance_id() != other.instance_id(),
               *self == *old(self),
   }
   ```

2. **`UniqueKeyValueToken<K, V>`**

   ```
   pub trait UniqueKeyValueToken<Key, Value>: KeyValueToken<Key, Value> {
       // Required method
       proof fn unique(tracked &mut self, tracked other: &Self)
       	ensures
               self.instance_id() != other.instance_id() || self.key() != other.key(),
               *self == *old(self),
   }
   ```

3. **`UniqueElementToken<V>`**

   ```
   pub trait UniqueElementToken<Element>: ElementToken<Element> {
       // Required method
       proof fn unique(tracked &mut self, tracked other: &Self)
           ensures
               self.instance_id() == other.instance_id() ==> self.element() != other.element(),
               *self == *old(self),
   }
   ```

4. **`UniqueSimpleToken`**

   ```
   pub trait UniqueSimpleToken: SimpleToken {
       // Required method
       proof fn unique(tracked &mut self, tracked other: &Self)
           ensures
               self.instance_id() != other.instance_id(),
               *self == *old(self),
   }
   ```

------

#### **Collection Structs**

1. **`MapToken<K, V, Token>`**

   ```
   pub struct MapToken<Key, Value, Token>where
       Token: KeyValueToken<Key, Value>,{ /* private fields */ }
   ```

   **Implementations**

   ```
   pub closed spec fn instance_id(self) -> InstanceId
   pub closed spec fn map(self) -> Map<Key, Value>
   pub open spec fn dom(self) -> Set<Key>
   	{ self.map().dom() }
   pub open spec fn spec_index(self, k: Key) -> Value
   	{ self.map()[k] }
   pub open spec fn index(self, k: Key) -> Value
   	{ self.map()[k] }
   pub proof fn empty(instance_id: InstanceId) -> tracked s : Self
       ensures
           s.instance_id() == instance_id,
           s.map() === Map::empty(),
   pub proof fn insert(tracked &mut self, tracked token: Token)
   	requires
           old(self).instance_id() == token.instance_id(),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.map() == old(self).map().insert(token.key(), token.value()),
   pub proof fn remove(tracked &mut self, key: Key) -> tracked token : Token
       requires
           old(self).map().dom().contains(key),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.map() == old(self).map().remove(key),
           token.instance_id() == self.instance_id(),
           token.key() == key,
           token.value() == old(self).map()[key],
   pub proof fn into_map(tracked self) -> tracked map : Map<Key, Token>
       ensures
           map.dom() == self.map().dom(),
           forall |key| {
               map.dom().contains(key)
                   ==> map[key].instance_id() == self.instance_id() && map[key].key() == key
                       && map[key].value() == self.map()[key]
           },
   pub proof fn from_map(instance_id: InstanceId, tracked map: Map<Key, Token>) -> tracked s : Self
       requires
           forall |key| #[trigger] map.dom().contains(key) ==> map[key].instance_id() == instance_id,
           forall |key| #[trigger] map.dom().contains(key) ==> map[key].key() == key,
       ensures
           s.instance_id() == instance_id,
           s.map().dom() == map.dom(),
           forall |key| #[trigger] map.dom().contains(key) ==> s.map()[key] == map[key].value(),
   ```

   

2. **`SetToken<V, Token>`**

   ```
   pub struct SetToken<Element, Token>where
       Token: ElementToken<Element>,{ /* private fields */ }
   ```

   **Implementations**

   ```
   pub closed spec fn instance_id(self) -> InstanceId
   pub closed spec fn set(self) -> Set<Element>
   pub open spec fn contains(self, element: Element) -> bool
   	{ self.set().contains(element) }
   pub proof fn empty(instance_id: InstanceId) -> tracked s : Self
       ensures
           s.instance_id() == instance_id,
           s.set() === Set::empty(),
   pub proof fn insert(tracked &mut self, tracked token: Token)
       requires
           old(self).instance_id() == token.instance_id(),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.set() == old(self).set().insert(token.element()),
   pub proof fn remove(tracked &mut self, element: Element) -> tracked token : Token
       requires
           old(self).set().contains(element),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.set() == old(self).set().remove(element),
           token.instance_id() == self.instance_id(),
           token.element() == element,
   pub proof fn into_map(tracked self) -> tracked map : Map<Element, Token>
       ensures
           map.dom() == self.set(),
           forall |key| {
               map.dom().contains(key)
                   ==> map[key].instance_id() == self.instance_id() && map[key].element() == key
           },
   pub proof fn from_map(instance_id: InstanceId, tracked map: Map<Element, Token>) -> tracked s : Self
       requires
           forall |key| #[trigger] map.dom().contains(key) ==> map[key].instance_id() == instance_id,
           forall |key| #[trigger] map.dom().contains(key) ==> map[key].element() == key,
       ensures
           s.instance_id() == instance_id,
           s.set() == map.dom(),
   ```

   

3. **`MultisetToken<V, Token>`**

   ```
   pub struct MultisetToken<Element, Token>where
       Token: ElementToken<Element>,{ /* private fields */ }
   ```

   **Implementations**

   ```
   pub closed spec fn instance_id(self) -> InstanceId
   pub closed spec fn multiset(self) -> Multiset<Element>
   pub proof fn empty(instance_id: InstanceId) -> tracked s : Self
       ensures
           s.instance_id() == instance_id,
           s.multiset() === Multiset::empty(),
   pub proof fn insert(tracked &mut self, tracked token: Token)
       requires
           old(self).instance_id() == token.instance_id(),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.multiset() == old(self).multiset().insert(token.element()),
   pub proof fn remove(tracked &mut self, element: Element) -> tracked token : Token
       requires
           old(self).multiset().contains(element),
       ensures
           self.instance_id() == old(self).instance_id(),
           self.multiset() == old(self).multiset().remove(element),
           token.instance_id() == self.instance_id(),
           token.element() == element,
   ```

​### PCell

------

#### **Overview**

The `PCell<T>` type (“permissioned call”) is the primitive Verus `Cell` type. It allows storing and modifying values while maintaining verification guarantees through a ghost permission token type `cell::PointsTo<V>`. The value is tracked by `cell::PointsTo<V>` with its `mem_contents()`. 

------

#### MemContents Implementations

```
// Represents (typed) contents of memory.
pub enum MemContents<T> {
    Uninit,
    Init(T),
}

pub open spec fn is_init(&self) -> bool
	{ self is Init }

pub open spec fn is_uninit(&self) -> bool
	{ self is Uninit }
pub open spec fn value(&self) -> T
	{ self->0 }
```

#### PCell Implementations

```
// A unique ID for the cell.
pub uninterp fn id(&self) -> CellId
//Return an empty (“uninitialized”) cell.
pub const exec fn empty() -> pt : (PCell<V>, Tracked<PointsTo<V>>)
	ensures
		pt.1@@ === pcell_points![pt.0.id() => MemContents::Uninit],
pub const exec fn new(v: V) -> pt : (PCell<V>, Tracked<PointsTo<V>>)
	ensures
		pt.1@@ === pcell_points![pt.0.id() => MemContents::Init(v)],
		//This is equivalent to
		pt.1@.id()==pt.0.id() && pt.1@.is_init() && pt.1@.value()==v
pub exec fn put(&self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<&mut PointsTo<V>>, v: V)
	requires
        old(perm)@ === pcell_points![self.id() => MemContents::Uninit],
        //This is equivalent to
		old(perm)@.id()==self.id() && old(perm)@.is_uninit()
	ensures
		perm@ === pcell_points![self.id() => MemContents::Init(v)],
pub exec fn take(&self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<&mut PointsTo<V>>) -> v : V
    requires
        self.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        old(perm).is_init(),
    ensures
        perm.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        perm.mem_contents() === MemContents::Uninit,
        v === old(perm).value(),
pub exec fn replace(&self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<&mut PointsTo<V>>, in_v: V) -> out_v : V
    requires
        self.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        old(perm).is_init(),
    ensures
        perm.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        perm.mem_contents() === MemContents::Init(in_v),
        out_v === old(perm).value(),
pub exec fn borrow<'a>(&'a self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<&'a PointsTo<V>>) -> v : &'a V
    requires
        self.id() === perm@.pcell,
        perm.is_init(),
    ensures
        *v === perm.value(),
pub exec fn into_inner(self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<PointsTo<V>>) -> v : V
    requires
        self.id() === perm@.pcell,
        perm.is_init(),
    ensures
        v === perm.value(),
//If V: Copy
pub exec fn write(&self, Tracked(perm): Tracked<&mut PointsTo<V>>, in_v: V)
    requires
        self.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        old(perm).is_init(),
    ensures
        perm.id() === old(perm)@.pcell,
        perm.mem_contents() === MemContents::Init(in_v),
```

------

#### cell::PointsTo Implementations

```
//Permission object associated with a PCell<V>.
pub struct PointsTo<V> { /* private fields */ }

//The CellId of the PCell this permission is associated with.
pub uninterp fn id(&self) -> CellId
//The contents of the cell, either unitialized or initialized to some V.
pub uninterp fn mem_contents(&self) -> MemContents<V>
pub open spec fn view(self) -> PointsToData<V>
{
    PointsToData {
        pcell: self.id(),
        value: option_from_mem_contents(self.mem_contents()),
    }
}
pub open spec fn is_init(&self) -> bool
	{ self.mem_contents().is_init() }
pub open spec fn is_uninit(&self) -> bool
	{ self.mem_contents().is_uninit() }
pub open spec fn value(&self) -> V
	recommends
        self.is_init(),
        { self.mem_contents().value() }
```

#### pcell_points

For a `cell::Pointsto<V>` perm, you may see propositions like `perm@==pcell_points![id => MemContents::Init(v)]`. This macro use @ to view `perm` as a `cell::PointsToData`, it is equivalent to `perm.id() == id && perm.is_init() && perm.value()==v`. Similarly, `perm@==pcell_points![id => MemContents::Uninit]` is equivalent to `perm.id() == id && perm.is_uninit()`.